    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1091">

                <pb facs="0973" n="961" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg961"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.52 Agrarian Commission/164: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Chief of the Division of the American
                        Republics</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bursley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the American
                        Commissioner, Agrarian Claims Commission</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Lawson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-13T14:00:00-04:00">June 13, 1940—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Reference our telephone conversation this morning. Quintanilla<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d1091-f1"/> informs me that Mexican Ambassador sent telegram no. 250
                    yesterday to Secretary of President Cárdenas indicating that Serrano apparently
                    still had not received promised instructions. Quintanilla suggested that if this
                    matter was not straightened out by the time you reach Mexico that you
                    communicate with me immediately whereupon on advice from me the Mexican
                    Ambassador would telephone to President Cárdenas.<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d1091-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herbert</hi> S. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">Bursley</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1091-f1" n="19" xml:id="d1091-f1-content">
                <p>Luis Quintanilla, Mexican Minister and Counselor of Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1091-f2" n="20" xml:id="d1091-f2-content">
                <p>In a memorandum of June 14, Mr. Bursley stated that Dr. Quintanilla had
                    telephoned him that the Ambassador had a message from President Cárdenas to the
                    effect that Serrano now had instructions permitting him to negotiate a global
                    settlement of the Agrarian Claims.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
